  "checkpoints": [
    {
      "at": 3.0,
      "prompt": "A is on the stack. Continue to follow one branch.",
      "learnerAction": "continue"
    },
    {
      "at": 6.4,
      "prompt": "Depth three: A, B, D. Will DFS open C next, or backtrack to A first?",
      "learnerAction": "answer"
    },
    {
      "at": 10.0,
      "prompt": "At C, both neighbours are already on the path. What should DFS do instead of pushing a node twice?",
      "learnerAction": "answer"
    },
    {
      "at": 13.8,
      "prompt": "The stack has shrunk back to A. Does that undo the visits to B and D?",
      "learnerAction": "answer"
    },
    {
      "at": 17.0,
      "prompt": "Continue to replay the visit order.",
      "learnerAction": "continue"
    }
  ],
  "narration": [
    {
      "at": 0.6,
      "text": "Four nodes, four edges. DFS starts at A and always follows one neighbour as deep as it can."
    },
    {
      "at": 2.2,
      "text": "Visit A and push it: the stack holds the path you are currently standing on."
    },
    {
      "at": 4.2,
      "text": "From A it takes B rather than C. The top of the stack is always the node you are at."
    },
    {
      "at": 6.2,
      "text": "B takes D. Three pushes deep, and nothing has been finished yet."
    },
    {
      "at": 8.2,
      "text": "D takes C. Depth four, with every step downward and none sideways."
    },
    {
      "at": 10.4,
      "text": "At C both neighbours are already on the path, so the visited check stops a second push."
    },
    {
      "at": 11.6,
      "text": "Then the pops: C leaves the stack, then D, then B. That is backtracking, not a new search."
    },
    {
      "at": 15.2,
      "text": "Back at A, the last neighbour C is already visited, so A pops too and the search ends."
    },
    {
      "at": 17.8,
      "text": "Replay of the visit order: A, then B, then D, then C. C is adjacent to A, yet DFS reached it the long way round."
    },
    {
      "at": 21.4,
      "text": "Depth first, not nearest first: that is why DFS is not a shortest-path search."
    }
  ],
  "fallback": {
    "text": "Static graph figure with nodes A, B, C, D and edges A-B, A-C, B-D, D-C, each node numbered in visit order: A=1, B=2, D=3, C=4. Beside it, the four stack snapshots as a table: [A], [A B], [A B D], [A B D C], then the unwind [A B D], [A B], [A], and empty. The table makes the two facts explicit: the frontier is last-in-first-out, and backtracking only pops the stack, it never unvisits a node."
  },
